  "motto": [
   "A garden lost. A city given.",
   "One promise, kept."
  ],
  "lead": [
   "The Bible is sixty-six books, written by many people over many centuries, and it tells one story. It begins with God and two people in a garden. It ends with God and a great crowd from every nation in a city. This study walks that story in eight movements and teaches one habit: before you read any verse, ask where you are in the story."
  ],
  "opening": [
   {
    "k": "quote",
    "r": "GEN.12.3",
    "label": "Genesis 12:3"
   }
  ],
  "frame": [
   "Most people meet the Bible one verse at a time: on a card, at a funeral, in a sermon, on a wall. That is not wrong, but it is like walking into a film halfway through and judging it by one scene. A promise in Genesis means more when you know how it was kept. A command at Mount Sinai sounds different once you know whom God had just rescued. A cry from the exile sounds different once you know there was a way home. So this study gives you three questions to ask of any passage. Where am I in the story? What has God promised by this point? What is still waiting to happen? It also gives you one thread to follow all the way through: God’s purpose for the nations, meaning all the peoples of the world, not one people only. Watch for it in every movement. It is there from the first page to the last."
  ],
  "selection": [
   "This is a selection, not a census. Eight movements is one way to divide the story, chosen because each is a place where Scripture itself turns. Others divide it differently. A common four-part summary is creation, fall, redemption (God buying back and setting free) and new creation. Craig Bartholomew and Michael Goheen, in ",
   {
    "i": "The Drama of Scripture"
   },
   {
    "chk": 1
   },
   ", tell it in six acts. All of these are maps of the same country. This study is the shelf’s map, so it walks no passage at length. Each movement reads a few verses and then points you to the study that reads that part of the story closely. It gives no dates for the earliest movements, because careful readers date Abraham, the exodus and the conquest differently. The Psalms, the wisdom books and most of the prophets sit inside these movements and have studies of their own."
  ],
  "close": {
   "title": "Finding your place in the story",
   "more": [
    {
     "s": "/mockups/the-good-news-directions/",
     "t": "The good news: how a person is made right with God"
    }
   ],
   "way": [
    "You have walked eight movements: creation and fall, the promise to Abraham, the exodus and Sinai, the land and the kingdom, exile and return, Christ, the church sent to the nations, and the new creation. Here is the habit to carry to the next passage you open. Ask where you are in the story. Ask what God has promised by that point. Ask what is still waiting. And watch for the nations, because from Babel to the city they are never out of God’s sight. A verse read this way is not a lonely saying on a card. It is a scene in a story God is telling, and keeping."
   ],
   "close": [
    "Nehemiah’s readers explained God’s law “so that the people could understand what was being read” (",
    {
     "r": "NEH.8.8",
     "t": "Nehemiah 8:8"
    },
    "). That is what this map is for. It does not replace reading. It tells you where you are standing when you read."
   ],
   "invite": [
    "The story has a place in it for you. It is not earned by being good enough, or by belonging to the right family or nation. God offers it freely to everyone who turns from their sin and trusts Jesus Christ, because, as the confession says, “There is no salvation apart from personal faith in Jesus Christ as Lord”. If you want to know what that means and how to respond, begin with The good news."
   ],
   "question": [
    "Which movement of the story do you most need to remember this week: the promise, the rescue, the exile, the return, or the city still to come?"
   ],
   "again": [
    "Read the passage again before you answer."
   ],
   "passage": "REV.21.1-5"
  }
